# MRP Planning Strategies and SAP Configuration

## 1. Production Planning Strategy

The PHOS Smart Lamp manufacturing project was designed using a **Make-to-Stock (MTS)** production strategy.

Under this approach, production is planned based on anticipated demand, allowing finished products to be manufactured and stored before customer orders are received.

The planning model was developed to coordinate raw material procurement, semi-finished product manufacturing, and final assembly.

## 2. Material Requirements Planning and Lot-Sizing Configuration

Different MRP and lot-sizing procedures were selected according to the material category and its role in the manufacturing process.

| Material Category | MRP Type | Lot-Sizing Procedure |
|---|---|---|
| Raw Materials (ROH) | VB – Manual Reorder Point Planning | FX – Fixed Lot Size |
| Semi-Finished Products (HALB) | PD – MRP Planning | EX – Lot-for-Lot |
| Finished Product (FERT) | PD – MRP Planning | EX – Lot-for-Lot |

### Raw Materials – VB / FX

Raw materials were planned using manual reorder point planning (VB).

The replenishment strategy was selected considering:

- Supplier delivery lead times.
- Supplier reliability.
- Procurement costs.
- Inventory holding costs.
- Expected material consumption.

A fixed lot-sizing procedure (FX) was selected, with procurement quantities established according to the material requirements of a production lot.

This approach was intended to maintain material availability while controlling purchasing and inventory costs.

### Semi-Finished Products – PD / EX

Internally manufactured semi-finished products were assigned deterministic material requirements planning (PD).

The lot-for-lot procedure (EX) was selected so that planned production quantities correspond to calculated requirements.

This configuration supports the coordination of intermediate manufacturing stages with the requirements generated by the finished product.

### Finished Product – PD / EX

The PHOS finished product was also assigned deterministic MRP planning (PD), combined with lot-for-lot sizing (EX).

The planning approach was designed to align finished-product production proposals with demand requirements while supporting the overall Make-to-Stock strategy.

## 3. Production Lot Dimensioning

The production lot size was determined through an engineering analysis of manufacturing capacity and operational constraints.

The study considered the available painting facility area, the space required per lamp, and the planned annual production volume.

| Parameter | Value |
|---|---:|
| Annual Production Volume | 6,930 units |
| Painting Facility Area | 60 m² |
| Area Required per Lamp | 0.21 m² |
| Theoretical Capacity | 285 units |
| Selected Production Lot | 231 units |
| Annual Production Lots | 30 |

The selected lot size of 231 units provided the basis for production scheduling and raw material replenishment calculations.

## 4. Raw Material Procurement Planning

Raw material procurement was organized around a reorder point strategy.

The purchasing analysis included:

- Reorder point determination.
- Procurement lot quantities.
- Supplier lead times.
- Material availability requirements.
- Procurement and inventory cost considerations.

The supplier analysis considered approximate average delivery times of:

- Domestic suppliers: 7 days.
- International suppliers: 15 days.

These parameters supported the definition of replenishment policies for the PHOS manufacturing system.

